From 157e0e71445fa23562dce160c95980387a039855 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Shammah Chancellor <shammah.chancellor@notvalid.com>
Date: Mon, 19 Oct 2026 04:08:06 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] logging: add RFC 5424 syslog output with typed JSON payload

Use daemon facility and standard severity values, RFC3339 time, and NILVALUE for unknown host/process identities and structured data. MSG uses a UTF8 BOM and generic JSON so typed event fields survive. This is a record format for the existing file/console sinks, not a new network destination.

35 logging/event cases pass. feature_logging passes after preserving the existing height-sensitive JSON test order. Vector 0.45 parse_syslog independently parsed all 214 actual startup/mining/shutdown sink records and their JSON payloads; no remote deployment is claimed.

(cherry picked from commit d723d3cef744f5810ecc028e781deafcce9c11ef)

N3-Source-Commit: d723d3cef744f5810ecc028e781deafcce9c11ef
N3-Source-Export: 0028

diff --git a/doc/logging.md b/doc/logging.md
index 6f00e4d45746749bb228b3fa2adafaa95d81851d..ab088bd5fbdd7e5bfe503f8ac029fd564a231a0e 100644
--- a/doc/logging.md
+++ b/doc/logging.md
@@ -71,3 +71,13 @@ values use the length-prefixed binary representation. Oversize entries use
 one sealed memfd via SCM_RIGHTS. Journal transport failures cannot indefinitely
 block the logger: socket sends have a 100ms timeout and stderr fallback.
 Source: [native journal protocol](https://systemd.io/JOURNAL_NATIVE_PROTOCOL/).
+
+`-logformat=syslog` writes RFC 5424 records to the usual file/console sinks;
+it does not open a remote network connection. It uses daemon facility 3 and
+the same severity mapping as journald. The header version is 1, with an
+RFC 3339 timestamp (or NILVALUE when timestamps are disabled). Unknown host,
+application, process, message-id and structured-data are represented by the
+specified NILVALUE, rather than made-up identity or enterprise identifiers.
+The UTF-8 MSG starts with a BOM and contains a generic JSON event, preserving
+all core and typed fields with JSON escaping. One physical line is one record.
+Source: [RFC 5424 sections 6 and 6.4](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc5424).
